The call site in question is inside `DeciderService.isTaskSkipped`:

```java
private boolean isTaskSkipped(WorkflowTask taskToSchedule, WorkflowModel workflow) {
    try {
        boolean isTaskSkipped = false;
        if (taskToSchedule != null) {
            TaskModel t = workflow.getTaskByRefName(taskToSchedule.getTaskReferenceName());
            if (t == null) {
                isTaskSkipped = false;
            } else if (t.getStatus().equals(SKIPPED)) {
                isTaskSkipped = true;
            }
        }
        return isTaskSkipped;
    } catch (Exception e) {
        throw new TerminateWorkflowException(e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

Here `t` is a `TaskModel` obtained from `workflow.getTaskByRefName(...)`. The code explicitly checks `if (t == null)` and only proceeds to `t.getStatus().equals(SKIPPED)` in the `else if` branch, i.e., only when `t` is guaranteed non-null.

However, `TaskModel.getStatus()` simply returns the field `status`:

```java
public Status getStatus() {
    return status;
}
```

